# <type>(<scope>): <imperative subject, 50 characters or fewer>
#
# Describe what changed in a short paragraph or bullets.
# Why: <reason supplied by the maintainer; omit for a trivial change>
#
# Types: docs, fix, update, style, ci; others when useful: chore, feat, refactor.
# Scopes: readme, policy-template, agents-template, disclosure-template,
# readme-template, contributing-template, pull-request-template,
# gitmessage-template, git, github, license.
# Pick one dominant scope or omit it. The vocabulary is preferred, not enforced.
# For a breaking change, add ! after the type or scope and explain it in the body.
#
# Add each applicable trailer as an uncommented line at the end, after a blank
# line. Repeat Assisted-by for multiple materially involved tools or models.
# Assisted-by: <tool>, <model identifier or not recorded> (<role or extent>)
# Checks-run: <check actually run> — <observed result>
# Ground-truth-source: <independent source of an added or changed reference value>
# Roles: plan, partial implementation, full implementation, review, refactor.
# Omit Checks-run when no check ran; omit Ground-truth-source when no reference
# value changed. A property test does not need a Ground-truth-source trailer.
#
# Record agent-made commits with Assisted-by, including edits to prose.
# Author-edited prose is disclosed at file or release level in the README.
# Git uses this file only for editor-based commits after commit.template is set;
# git commit -m bypasses it. Review the final message, including squash commits.
